1:["$","$Ld",null,{"page":{"id":"phase-7--7d-going-native","kind":"phase-article","route":"/roadmap/phase-7/7d-going-native","sourceUrl":"https://refold.la/roadmap/phase-7/7d-going-native","sourceHash":"d657fd497b5ec4607f4b37944426ff7f62d60359a716bfdeac6fd86a3ee07e54","collectedAt":"2026-09-01T23:12:10.256Z","bodyMode":"authorized-full-body","title":{"en":"7D — Going Native","zh":"7D——达到母语者水平"},"description":{"en":"","zh":""},"segments":[{"id":"what-youre-doing","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"What You're Doing","zh":"你正在做什么"}},{"id":"segment-0002","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"This is the final phase — and it's a lifelong one. The goal is to close the remaining gap between a highly fluent non-native speaker and someone who grew up in the language. That gap exists in three areas: vocabulary, cultural depth, and the kind of deep intuitive connection that comes from living in a language for decades.","zh":"这是最后一个阶段，而且是终身阶段。目标是缩小高度流利的非母语者与从小就在这种语言环境中长大的人之间的剩余差距。这种差距存在于三个方面：词汇量、文化深度，以及只有在一种语言中生活数十年才能形成的深层直觉联系。"}},{"id":"segment-0003","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Not everyone needs or wants to reach this level. If functional fluency is your goal, you've already achieved it. But if you want to be indistinguishable from a native speaker — not as a performance, but as a reality — this is the path.","zh":"并不是每个人都需要或希望达到这个水平。如果你的目标是实用流利，你已经实现了它。但如果你想做到与母语者无法区分——不是为了表现，而是成为现实——这就是你要走的路。"}},{"id":"segment-0004","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Be honest with yourself about what this requires. Native speakers have spent their entire lives in the language. They've had tens of thousands of experiences, read hundreds of books, and had millions of conversations. You're compressing decades of lived experience into several thousand hours of deliberate effort, probably less than 10% of the time a 29-year-old native speaker has spent.","zh":"请诚实面对这一目标的要求。母语者在这种语言中度过了整个人生。他们积累了数万次经历，读过数百本书，进行过数百万次对话。而你正试图用几千小时的刻意努力压缩数十年的生活经验，这可能还不到一名29岁母语者投入时间的10%。"}},{"id":"segment-0005","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"It's worth noting that this is <strong>basically impossible</strong> to do if you don't live in a country or community that speaks the target language.","zh":"值得注意的是，如果你不生活在使用目标语言的国家或社区中，这件事<strong>基本上不可能</strong>做到。"}},{"id":"what-youre-working-on","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"What You're Working On","zh":"你正在训练什么"}},{"id":"segment-0007","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Vocabulary expansion</strong> — A college-educated native speaker knows 15,000-20,000 words (depending how you count). You probably know 10,000-12,000 at this point. The remaining words aren't the \"common\" ones — they're the long tail: the word for shoelace, the name of that specific type of wrench, the verb for what birds do when they clean their feathers.","zh":"<strong>词汇扩展</strong>——受过大学教育的母语者掌握15,000—20,000个单词（取决于统计方式）。到了这个阶段，你可能已经掌握了10,000—12,000个。剩下的并不是那些“常见”词，而是长尾词汇：鞋带的说法、某种特定扳手的名称、鸟类清洁羽毛时所做动作对应的动词。"}},{"id":"segment-0008","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The best way to learn them is to live your life in the language and actively notice when you don't know something. Be excessively curious. Ask the natives around you about every little thing. <em>\"What was that word?\" \"Why'd you say it like that?\" \"What would you call this thing?\"</em> Carrying a notebook around with you to write down new things can be helpful.","zh":"学习这些词的最佳方式，是用这种语言生活，并主动留意自己何时遇到了不认识的东西。保持极强的好奇心。向身边的母语者询问每一件小事。<em>“刚才那个词是什么？”“你为什么要那样说？”“这个东西你们怎么称呼？”</em>随身带一本笔记本，记下新发现，也会很有帮助。"}},{"id":"segment-0009","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Reading widely is the single most effective way to expand your vocabulary at this level — books use a far wider range of words than spoken language.","zh":"在这个阶段，广泛阅读是扩展词汇量最有效的单一方法——书面语使用的词汇范围远比口语广。"}},{"id":"segment-0010","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Cultural literacy</strong> — Native speakers share a body of cultural references: childhood TV shows, historical events from their country's perspective, proverbs, jokes, songs, and shared experiences. You can't absorb this passively — seek it out. Watch the shows that everyone in the culture grew up watching (especially those in your age/peer group). Read the books that are assigned in school. Learn the history from the perspective of native sources.","zh":"<strong>文化素养</strong>——母语者共享一套文化参照：童年时期的电视节目、从本国视角出发的历史事件、谚语、笑话、歌曲和共同经历。你无法被动吸收这些内容——要主动去寻找。观看这个文化群体中每个人都从小看到的节目（尤其是你这个年龄段或同龄人群体熟悉的节目）。阅读学校布置的书籍。从母语者的资料和视角中学习历史。"}},{"id":"segment-0011","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Fluidity and depth</strong> — Being able to describe a complex feeling or thought without hesitation, in multiple ways, with the exact right nuance — this is what separates \"very fluent\" from \"native.\" The only path is time, practice, and a deep commitment to using the language as your primary mode of thought and expression. Chorusing, shadowing, and extensive reading all help, but there's no shortcut. <strong>This takes years</strong>.","zh":"<strong>流畅度与深度</strong>——能够毫不犹豫地从多个角度描述一种复杂的感受或想法，并准确传达其中的细微差别，这正是“非常流利”与“母语者”之间的区别。唯一的道路是时间、练习，以及坚定地把这种语言作为主要思维和表达方式。跟读、影子跟读和广泛阅读都有帮助，但没有捷径。<strong>这需要数年时间</strong>。"}},{"id":"segment-0012","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Emotional connection</strong> — Native speakers don't just <em>use</em> their language. They <em>feel</em> in it. Jokes land differently, sad stories hit harder, and certain words carry weight that doesn't translate. Building this kind of connection requires deep engagement with the culture and the people who speak the language.","zh":"<strong>情感联系</strong>——母语者不只是<em>使用</em>他们的语言。他们在其中<em>感受</em>世界。笑话听起来会有不同的效果，悲伤的故事会更令人触动，而某些词承载的分量是无法翻译的。建立这种联系，需要深入参与这种语言的文化以及与使用这种语言的人交往。"}},{"id":"your-routine","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Your Routine","zh":"你的日常安排"}},{"id":"segment-0014","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"This phase doesn't have a strict routine. You're living in the language. But here's a general framework:","zh":"这个阶段没有严格的日程安排。你正在用这种语言生活。不过，下面是一个大致框架："}},{"id":"segment-0015","type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"en":"<strong>Priming:</strong> 10%","zh":"<strong>预热：</strong>10%"},{"en":"<strong>Interactive/Freeflow Immersion:</strong> 70%","zh":"<strong>互动式/自由流沉浸：</strong>70%"},{"en":"<strong>Output:</strong> 20%","zh":"<strong>输出：</strong>20%"}]},{"id":"sample-daily-schedule","type":"heading","level":3,"content":{"en":"Sample Daily Schedule","zh":"每日安排示例"}},{"id":"segment-0017","type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"en":"Vocab study (mostly review): 15 mins","zh":"词汇学习（主要是复习）：15分钟"},{"en":"Sentence Mining or active reading: 35 mins","zh":"句子挖掘或主动阅读：35分钟"},{"en":"Freeflow Reading: 45 mins","zh":"自由流阅读：45分钟"},{"en":"Freeflow Listening: 45 mins","zh":"自由流听力：45分钟"},{"en":"Speaking with Partner, Monologuing, or Writing: 40 mins","zh":"与伙伴练习口语、独白或写作：40分钟"}]},{"id":"when-are-you-done","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"When Are You Done?","zh":"什么时候算完成？"}},{"id":"segment-0019","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"You're \"done\" when:","zh":"当你满足以下条件时，就算“完成”："}},{"id":"segment-0020","type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"en":"You know 15,000+ words","zh":"掌握15,000个以上的单词"},{"en":"You can think and feel in the target language without effort","zh":"能够毫不费力地用目标语言思考和感受"},{"en":"Native speakers are surprised to learn you aren't a native speaker, even after extended conversation","zh":"即使经过长时间交谈，母语者仍会惊讶地得知你并非母语者"}]},{"id":"segment-0021","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Realistically, this phase takes years. But at this point, the language isn't a project anymore — it's part of who you are. You'll never truly be done learning (poetic, isn't it?)","zh":"现实地说，这个阶段需要数年时间。但到了这一步，语言已经不再是一个项目，而是你身份的一部分。你永远不会真正结束学习（很有诗意，不是吗？）"}},{"id":"research-and-reasoning","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Research and Reasoning","zh":"研究与依据"}},{"id":"segment-0023","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"This final phase acknowledges the limitations of the traditional \"native speaker\" concept, a concept challenged by <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-1770.1992.tb01044.x\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Cook's (1992)</a> work on multicompetence. Cook argues that highly proficient L2 speakers develop a distinct and valid form of linguistic competence — different from monolingual native speakers, but not deficient. The goal of this phase isn't to erase that difference but to close the gap to the point where it no longer matters in practice.","zh":"这个最终阶段承认传统“母语者”概念的局限性；<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-1770.1992.tb01044.x\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Cook（1992）</a>关于多元能力的研究对这一概念提出了挑战。Cook认为，高度熟练的第二语言使用者会发展出一种独特且有效的语言能力形式——它不同于单语母语者的能力，但并非有所欠缺。本阶段的目标不是抹去这种差异，而是将差距缩小到实践中不再重要的程度。"}},{"id":"segment-0024","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The vocabulary targets reflect <a href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/learning-vocabulary-in-another-language/491314AA1B451AD04F3536000F1C9F0D\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Nation's (2001)</a> research on native-speaker vocabulary size. Goulden, Nation, and Read (1990) estimated that university-educated native speakers know approximately 17,000–20,000 word families. The 15,000+ target for this phase reflects what research suggests is sufficient for high-level literacy and sophisticated discourse — close enough to native-like knowledge that gaps in lived experience, rather than vocabulary, become the primary remaining limitation.","zh":"词汇目标反映了<a href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/learning-vocabulary-in-another-language/491314AA1B451AD04F3536000F1C9F0D\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Nation（2001）</a>关于母语者词汇量的研究。Goulden、Nation和Read（1990）估计，受过大学教育的母语者掌握大约17,000—20,000个词族。本阶段设定15,000个以上的目标，体现了研究所指出的、足以支持高水平读写能力和复杂话语的词汇量——这种水平已经足够接近母语者的知识，以至于剩余的主要限制不再是词汇，而是生活经验的差距。"}}],"cards":[]}}]
